Decide on a clear error or default for empty input and state it in the docstring.</span></li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>The short answer</h2>\n  <p>An average of nothing has no sensible value, so the function has to do something on purpose when it gets an empty list. For <code>mean(xs)</code>, the best move is to pick that behaviour (here, raise <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>) and write it in the docstring, so every caller knows what to expect. That is the habit of <em>defining the empty-input contract</em>.</p>\n\n  <h2>Where the answer went wrong</h2>\n  <p>Returning <code>None</code> feels safe: nothing crashes, and the empty case is \"handled\". The catch is the word <em>broad</em>. A <code>try/except</code> that catches every exception does not only catch the empty list. It catches anything that goes wrong on that line, and turns each problem into the same quiet <code>None</code>.</p>\n  <p>It also moves the failure instead of removing it. <code>None</code> flows on to the next line, and the crash shows up later, somewhere else, with a message about <code>None</code> and no hint about the empty list that caused it. Option B does decide something, but "}}]}}
